AI Reviewby AI Reviewer ()27 Jul 2026, 19:59 (modified: 25 Sept 2026, 05:32)Program Chairs, AI Reviewer, Area Chairs, Senior Program Committee, Authors, Program Committee Submitted[Revisions](https://openreview.net/revisions?id=hD8xCDWz57)

**Review:**

**Title:** Inference-Time Latent Memory Management for Long-Horizon LLM Agents

**Synopsis of the paper**\
The paper adds a bounded, session-local memory bank to MemGen so that dynamically generated latent memories can persist beyond their immediate use. Stored memories are retrieved to condition the Weaver, which regenerates a query-specific latent representation for the Reasoner without parameter updates. EventQA experiments compare this mechanism with recent-text, summarization, and textual-retrieval controls, alongside query-time ablations and efficiency measurements.

**Summary of Review**\
The paper isolates a concrete problem in generated latent memory: lifecycle management after a representation has been produced. Its most distinctive contribution is Weaver-mediated regeneration from retrieved memories, rather than latent storage itself. The experiments show higher mean exact match and fewer parser failures than the evaluated controls, but they do not isolate improved long-horizon evidence use from answer-selection and formatting effects. The contribution is also narrower in light of omitted memory-management precedents and absent comparisons with cited latent-memory systems. Low absolute performance, limited evaluation scope, and underspecified inference details further constrain the conclusions.

**Strengths**

- The ablation design directly tests the paper’s principal integration choice. Figure 3 compares Weaver conditioning with both discarded retrieval results and direct latent injection, producing large differences in exact match and format failures; this demonstrates that the choice of latent-memory integration materially changes system behavior.
- The evaluation protocol controls cross-question contamination. The Experimental Setup states that the bank is constructed during context processing, frozen before question answering, and restored from the same snapshot for every question, which is important for interpreting performance as reuse of context-derived memory rather than accumulation of answer-time information.
- The reported metrics expose distinct aspects of system behavior. Figures 2–3 jointly report exact match, raw-output answer recall, and parser failures, making visible that retrieving or mentioning the gold event is distinct from producing an exactly scorable answer.

**Weaknesses**

- The main results do not isolate better long-horizon evidence use from answer-selection and formatting effects. The Evaluation Metrics section defines recall as whether the gold event appears in raw output; in Figure 2, Dense E5 has higher recall than the proposed method,  versus , while the proposed method has much higher exact match,  versus , and far fewer format failures,  versus . Thus, the evidence establishes better exact-answer production under the benchmark parser, but not that the gain arises from retrieving or retaining relevant historical information more effectively; parse-conditional accuracy and retrieval/selection/format error decomposition are needed.
- The memory-management novelty is positioned without two relevant precedents. EM-LLM provides training-free online episodic storage with similarity-based and temporally contiguous retrieval, while Memoria develops explicit retention, retrieval, and forgetting mechanisms (Fountas et al., 2025; Park & Bak, 2024); neither work is cited anywhere in the paper. These methods do not subsume Weaver-mediated regeneration, but they narrow the lifecycle-management novelty and make that regeneration interface the more clearly differentiated contribution.
- The empirical evaluation does not establish performance relative to persistent latent-memory systems. The Recurrent and Retrieved Latent State section cites MEMORYLLM as maintaining a self-updatable latent pool and M+ as adding trained retrieval, but Figures 2–3 compare only textual controls and internal ablations (Wang et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2025). The results therefore do not determine how the proposed inference-only mechanism compares with the latent-memory approaches identified as its closest architectural context.
- The headline performance remains low and lacks a reference ceiling. Figure 2 reports  exact match and a mean of  format failures among  questions, using one checkpoint and the same five EventQA contexts across seeded passes; no chance level, gold-evidence oracle, or long-context upper bound is reported. Consequently, the relative gain cannot be placed against attainable performance, and the experiments provide limited evidence for the broader long-horizon-agent framing.
- The contribution of temporal decay is neither statistically nor operationally established. Figure 3 shows an exact-match margin of only  over no decay, smaller than the no-decay run standard deviation of , while no decay has higher recall; additionally, the method equations update  after retrieval, whereas the Experimental Setup calls query-time evaluation read-only without specifying whether this metadata and the global counter are restored between questions. These facts leave both the reliability and precise meaning of the decay ablation unresolved.
- The central frozen-Weaver operation is insufficiently specified in the main text. The equation  does not state how a variable-size retrieved set is ordered, concatenated, masked, or otherwise supplied to the unchanged Weaver, nor why Reasoner-derived queries and Weaver-space keys are directly cosine-comparable. Because this interface defines both the proposed mechanism and the direct-injection ablation, its tensor-level construction is necessary for technical assessment.

**Suggestions for Improvement**

- Performance as a function of evidence age would test the long-horizon premise more directly. Stratifying questions by source position, retrieval distance, slot refresh, and eviction history would show whether improvements concentrate on information that recent-text conditioning can no longer retain.
- An amortization analysis would make the efficiency result applicable beyond the fixed EventQA protocol. Figure 4 averages construction costs over  questions per context and reports incremental GPU allocation while latent slots reside on CPU; varying the number of questions and separating construction, query, CPU-storage, and total GPU costs would expose the break-even regime.
- The exposition would be clearer if the two-phase workflow preceded the memory equations. A short EventQA example followed by context-time construction, bank freezing, independent query-time retrieval, and reset would define “session-local” concretely and make the experimental protocol easier to follow.

**Additional Details and Minor Issues**

- The abstract contains the typo “rretrieves.”
- The Method section alternates between  and , and between  and  for stored memories.
- The retrieval candidate set contains memories, whereas the bank is formally defined as a set of triples.
- Because  is assigned on insertion and refresh, “last-access count” is more accurate than “last-retrieval count.”
- The abstract’s “matched no-bank path” is ambiguous because the body defines both recent-text and Matched-16 no-bank conditions.
- The Matched-16 description should report how often its exact-token rendering requirement rejects a run.

